# mail-engine/docker/postfix/header_checks — what gets stripped on the way out.
#
# Every one of these removes something that leaks the sender's private network into a message that
# goes to a stranger. A Received header from a submitting client carries the client's LAN IP and
# hostname; User-Agent and X-Mailer name the software and version; X-Originating-IP is the address
# of the person who wrote the message.
#
# IGNORE, not REJECT: the header is removed and the message continues. A rule here that rejected
# would drop real mail because of a header nobody cares about.

# The Received line added for a client that authenticated to submission. Postfix only applies
# header_checks to submitted mail via local_header_rewrite_clients, so inbound Received chains — the
# ones a receiver needs in order to trace a message — are left intact.
/^Received:.*with ESMTPSA/            IGNORE
/^X-Originating-IP:/                  IGNORE
/^X-Mailer:/                          IGNORE
/^User-Agent:/                        IGNORE
/^X-Enigmail:/                        IGNORE

# Headers a client must never be able to set for itself: they are the receiver's evidence about
# authentication, and a forged one is worse than none.
/^Authentication-Results:/            IGNORE
/^ARC-(Seal|Message-Signature|Authentication-Results):/  IGNORE
